/* "Self-drawing glyphs" effect for the Fonts page hero: a dark ink dot
   traces out a letterform — like a type designer sketching a glyph — then
   the finished letter holds for a beat and fades away, before a new one
   starts somewhere else, with up to three on screen together. The exact
   same technique as the Work page's math-bg.js, but instead of Lucide
   icons, every outline here is real glyph geometry extracted straight out
   of this page's own six custom fonts (Sheikah/Hylian/Gerudo/Fez/Tunic/
   Unown — see src/assets/fonts/*.otf and the font-sample cards below), via
   fontTools' SVGPathPen. So the hero literally shows the page's own
   scripts being written — a different, randomly chosen letter each time
   (all 26 for most fonts), not just a fixed handful repeating.

   Each glyph is normalized to a shared 24-unit-tall box (its own natural
   width preserved, not forced square — a "w"/"h" pair travels with every
   entry below), the same technique Lucide icons use, capped at a 2:1
   width:height aspect ratio so a naturally very flat/wide glyph doesn't
   stretch into an oddly long strip.

   Tunic is a special case: rather than 26 separate letters, its pool below
   (TUNIC_LIGATURES) is the font's own real GSUB "liga" ligature glyphs —
   ch/sh/th/dh/ng/dj and the vowel/rhotic pairs (ae/ai/au/ei/ii/oi/ou,
   ar/er/ir/or/xr) the font's actual phonetic spelling scheme combines into
   one drawn shape (see the pangram on this page: "dha kwik... djamps...
   leizii..." — each of those digraphs is one such ligature). Extracted by
   glyph name (e.g. "C_H.liga") straight out of the font's GSUB table
   rather than picked apart and repositioned by hand, so each renders
   exactly as the font itself draws that combination — not two separate
   letterforms placed side by side.

   The reveal itself needs no hand-rolled path-walking: every SVG shape here
   is an SVGGeometryElement, so the browser's own getTotalLength()/
   getPointAtLength() drive a standard stroke-dasharray/dashoffset "line
   drawing" animation, same as math-bg.js. Ink is dark and faint (not the
   shared opaque white) because this hero's background (.hero--fonts) is a
   light parchment gradient, not the usual mid-to-dark brand gradient, and
   because this is meant to read as background texture rather than content
   competing with the hero heading or the font cards below.

   Which font draws next isn't uniformly random — see refillQueue() below —
   it cycles through all 6 fonts once (each in a random order) before
   repeating, so no font can appear two or three times in a row purely by
   chance the way a flat random pick occasionally would.

   Performance constraints mirror math-bg.js/trail-bg.js:
   - Only runs where an <svg class="hero-glyphs"> exists (Fonts page only).
   - Skips entirely under prefers-reduced-motion.
   - Pauses via IntersectionObserver when the hero is off-screen, and via
     visibilitychange when the tab isn't active.
   - At most a handful of glyphs exist at once (oldest dropped once the cap
     is hit); each is a few real DOM nodes updated per frame.
   - The <svg> is sized purely by CSS (see .hero-glyphs, position:absolute;
     inset:0) — no devicePixelRatio bookkeeping needed since it's vector.
     Resize only needs to re-read the hero's current box; watched via
     ResizeObserver on the hero itself, matching the other hero effects. */
